 Follow this workflow:
 1. Read the problem: carefully read the problem statement, identify the input format, output format, constraints, and every example given in the problem.
-2. Plan an algorithm: before writing code, decide on an efficient algorithm that fits the constraints, and reason about its time and space complexity. Consider edge cases.
+2. Plan an algorithm: before writing code, decide on an efficient algorithm and explicitly state its time complexity and space complexity. Compare the stated time complexity against the input constraints (e.g. n up to 10^5 or 2*10^5 implies roughly O(n log n) at most; a 10^9-scale bound rules out anything but O(log n) or O(1) per query). If the algorithm would be too slow for the constraints, pick a better one before writing any code. Consider edge cases.
 3. Implement it: write the solution in the appropriate file(s) in the repository. If the task is a method of a given `Solution` class, implement the method in that class; otherwise write a complete program that reads standard input and writes standard output.
 4. Test it: run the solution on the examples given in the problem statement and compare its output with the expected output exactly. If any example fails, debug and fix the solution, then re-run the examples. Also test a few small edge cases and, if useful, a larger random input to check performance.
 5. Stress test the running time: generate a maximum-size input that satisfies the constraints stated in the problem (largest n, largest values, worst-case structure), run your solution on it, and measure the running time (e.g. with `time`). Compare it against the time limit stated in the problem (or a typical limit of about 1-2 seconds if none is given). If it is too slow, optimize the algorithm (better complexity, fewer Python-level loops, faster I/O) and repeat this step.
